# Climate, fan and water-heater controls

Someone adjusting comfort needs to know both the current reading and what the equipment has been asked to do. A thermostat mode, fan preset, humidity target and hot-water programme are different controls and must not be merged into one generic dial.

**HA-CTRL-005 — reference design specification.** This specifies the local design coverage requested by HA-GAP-005 for an existing Home Assistant capability. The examples do not install a native adapter, connect to a backend or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-005-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Domain | Required distinctions and capability gates |
| --- | --- |
| Climate | `current_temperature` versus target; selected HVAC mode versus reported `hvac_action`; single target versus supported lower/upper targets. |
| Fan | On/off, percentage, preset, oscillation and direction are separate capabilities. A preset name must remain the supplied name. |
| Humidifier/dehumidifier | Measured humidity versus target; device class identifies adding or removing moisture. Do not reverse the meaning to fit an icon. |
| Water heater | Reported operation mode, available target and current measurement. Offer only supplied controls; retain equipment-specific instructions and protections. |
| Every numeric target | Native unit, limits, increment and precision; validate dual-target constraints before any request. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-005-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Selected Heat with reported Idle is valid: it does not mean a fault or active heating. Off is not unavailable. Keep a known target visible when a current measurement is missing, with separate labels. If the target is not known, do not fill it with the measured temperature.

A requested target stays a draft or pending value until the documented confirmation condition is met. If modes change during an edit, do not send a target to a now-unsupported mode. Preserve the entered draft for review where the application actually does so. For a rejected target, identify the range error at the field; for a failed request, identify the task-level failure. Avoid diagnosing a boiler, refrigerant or air-quality fault from an unconfirmed UI request.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-005-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Use “Measured temperature”, “Target temperature”, “Selected mode” and “Reported activity” where ambiguity would otherwise arise. Keep °C/°F and percentage units associated with each value. Humidity settings are not a medical assessment. Water-heater recipes must defer to equipment instructions and competent advice; this design system does not prescribe a safe temperature or alter protective programmes.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-005-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Expose target values and modes as labelled native controls, with keyboard adjustment and readable units. Do not require a drag around a circular thermostat to enter a precise value. Announce target changes once, separately from frequent temperature readings. Keep both setpoints readable at enlarged text; never hide one behind a tooltip or colour alone.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-005-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Use native Climate, Fan, Humidifier and Water heater controls and their actual supported modes/features. Preserve the entity's unit conversion and precision. The pinned climate source in the gap inventory is source evidence, not live verification of every device. These examples are design recipes; no new thermostat, fan service or hot-water automation is installed by this documentation.

